                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3bc8a098-c00a-3c22-8d07-7104027325c4",
                                        "entry_id": "02cd1a58-50af-326f-9918-53ae29a593e6",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Brought low; degraded."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fbdba03c-be0f-4d2d-a203-d2719e8e03d5",
                                                "sense_id": "3bc8a098-c00a-3c22-8d07-7104027325c4",
                                                "text": "8), Azure, a thistle ensigned with an Imperial Crown, all proper; and its use is pretty frequent in the somewhat debased heraldry of the close of the last century and the beginning of the present."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "46863db8-d2bd-41d5-81a7-e4ef14b20f64",
                                                "sense_id": "3bc8a098-c00a-3c22-8d07-7104027325c4",
                                                "text": "58,) and also a new crest, viz. upon a wreath argent and vert, a demi-dragon erased gules P, gorged about the loins with a ducal coronet ... 59,) are an admirable specimen of the complex and debased heraldry of the day. 1515."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7e67a923-9388-4a54-9ecb-2b8a03673d5e",
                                                "sense_id": "3bc8a098-c00a-3c22-8d07-7104027325c4",
                                                "text": "The badge itself is a remarkable medley of objects, and another good example of the debased heraldry of Tudor days. It represents a castle with the crowned hawthorn tree of the Tudors growing in the lower ward behind the principal[…]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "557b76fa-ae06-4e08-8ace-b261a80f1750",
                                                "sense_id": "3bc8a098-c00a-3c22-8d07-7104027325c4",
                                                "text": "DEBASED HERALDRY. Of debased heraldry there is no lack of examples, and a few are ancient. Thomas de Insula, bishop of Ely (1345-61), bore gules, three bezants, on each a crowned king, robed sable, doubled ermine, sustaining[…]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1d117cf0-c760-3a09-a76f-f6621c70ba04",
                                        "entry_id": "02cd1a58-50af-326f-9918-53ae29a593e6",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Abased, abaissé: (of a charge) borne lower than usual."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "75f8eed2-bec9-4f51-8102-b0c4bfd11d2a",
                                                "sense_id": "1d117cf0-c760-3a09-a76f-f6621c70ba04",
                                                "text": "For quotations using this term, see Citations:debased."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
